"g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.817381,"lo":172.949028,"d":2052,"wp":"en:Takapūneke","ds":"Takapūneke, with the location also known as Red House Bay, is a former kāinga—an unfortified Māori village—adjacent to present-day Akaroa, New Zealand. Takapūneke was a major trading post for the local iwi (tribe), Ngāi Tahu, as there was safe anchorage for European vessels.","wpu":"Takapūneke","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Remote_view_of_Takap%C5%ABneke_%28to_the_right_of_Akaroa_residences%29.jpg/960px-Remote_view_of_Takap%C5%ABneke_%28to_the_right_of_Akaroa_residences%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Akaroa Heritage park","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.799281,"lo":172.997967,"d":2487},{"n":"Frank Worsley's Homestead","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":-43.806288,"lo":173.003857,"d":2881},{"n":"Edge View","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.820446,"lo":173.005383,"d":3456},{"n":"Sandy Bay","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.77515,"lo":172.955654,"d":3463},{"n":"Skyline Beech Lookout","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.806581,"lo":173.012717,"d":3592},{"n":"Ōnuku","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.835611,"lo":172.946915,"d":3801},{"n":"Ōpukutahi","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.807846,"lo":172.918727,"d":3966},{"n":"Ferngully","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.809864,"lo":173.024634,"d":4576},{"n":"Look Out","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.812144,"lo":173.02595,"d":4717},{"n":"Ōnawe","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.77364,"lo":172.926704,"d":4811},{"n":"Shamarra Alpacas Farm","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.791068,"lo":172.91118,"d":4816},{"n":"Ōnawe Pā","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.770829,"lo":172.926464,"d":5055},{"n":"Mikimiki Knob","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.81141,"lo":173.034959,"d":5420},{"n":"Boat houses","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.757131,"lo":172.943287,"d":5680},{"n":"Akaroa Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":-43.808487,"lo":172.963572,"d":525},{"n":"Akaroa library","g":"essentials","k":"library","la":-43.809999,"lo":172.963103,"d":680,"h":"Mo-Fr 10:00-16:30; Sa 10:00-13:00","wifi":1},{"n":"Akaroa Library & Cinema","g":"essentials","k":"wifi","la":-43.810052,"lo":172.963182,"d":681,"wifi":1},{"n":"Takamatua","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.783333,"lo":172.970833,"d":2419,"ds":"Takamatua, formerly known as German Bay, is a small town situated in Akaroa Harbour on Banks Peninsula in New Zealand. The main road to Akaroa (State Highway 75) passes through this locality. It is 3 kilometres (1.9 mi) north of Akaroa township.","wpu":"Takamatua","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Takamatua_Bay.jpg/960px-Takamatua_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ōtūtereinga","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.81324,"lo":172.91186,"d":4597,"ds":"Ōtūtereinga is a cliff at the northern end of Wainui, on the western side of Akaroa Harbour. Its name is also spelt Otutereinga and O Tu Te Reinga. \"Ō\" means \"place of\", \"tu\" translates to \"stand\", \"te\" signifies \"the\", and \"reinga\" refers to the \"flitting place from which spirits transition to the underworld\", similar to Cape Reinga.","wpu":"Ōtūtereinga","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/%C5%8Ct%C5%ABtereinga.jpg/960px-%C5%8Ct%C5%ABtereinga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Banks Track","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.852957,"lo":172.964386,"d":5340,"ds":"The Banks Track is a 31-kilometre privately owned walking track on Banks Peninsula in the Canterbury Region of the South Island of New Zealand. The track opened in 1989 as the first privately owned track in New Zealand.","wpu":"Banks Track","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Banks_Peninsula_Track_0348.jpg/960px-Banks_Peninsula_Track_0348.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Akaroa Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.8446,"lo":172.9252,"d":5583,"ds":"Akaroa Harbour is part of Banks Peninsula in the Canterbury region of New Zealand. The harbour enters from the southern coast of the peninsula, heading in a predominantly northerly direction. It is one of two major inlets in Banks Peninsula, on the coast of Canterbury, New Zealand; the other is Lyttelton Harbour on the northern coast.","wpu":"Akaroa Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/AkaroaHarbourDec032024_02.jpg/960px-AkaroaHarbourDec032024_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ellangowan Scenic Reserve","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.788,"lo":173.038,"d":5927,"ds":"Ellangowan Scenic Reserve is a public scenic reserve on Banks Peninsula, New Zealand, near Akaroa at the head of Hickory Bay. It is notable for having the northernmost naturally-occurring red beech (Nothofagus fusca) on Banks Peninsula.","wpu":"Ellangowan Scenic Reserve","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ellengowan_Scenic_Reserve_Aug_2025_MRD_10.jpg/960px-Ellengowan_Scenic_Reserve_Aug_2025_MRD_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Robinsons Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.75,"lo":172.95,"d":6284,"ds":"Robinsons Bay is a settlement and bay in Akaroa Harbour on Banks Peninsula, New Zealand. Robinsons Bay was first settled in 1850 and was exploited for milling, and once the timber had run out it was converted into farming pasture.","wpu":"Robinsons Bay","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Robinsons_Bay_map.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Duvauchelle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.7498,"lo":172.9321,"d":6781,"ds":"Duvauchelle (Māori: Kaitouna) is a small town situated at the head of Akaroa Harbour on Banks Peninsula in New Zealand. State Highway 75 passes through the town. The Ōnawe Peninsula separates Duvauchelle bay from Barry's Bay.","wpu":"Duvauchelle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Duvauchelle_Post_Office_%28c.1921%29.JPG/960px-Duvauchelle_Post_Office_%28c.1921%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Otepatotu Scenic Reserve","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.749,"lo":173.017,"d":7365,"ds":"Otepatotu Scenic Reserve is a public scenic reserve on Banks Peninsula, New Zealand, between Akaroa and Le Bons Bay. It has a stand of regenerating mountain tōtara (Podocarpus laetus) considered to be one of the finest remaining on Banks Peninsula.","wpu":"Otepatotu Scenic Reserve","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Otepatotu_MRD_15.jpg/960px-Otepatotu_MRD_15.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pōhatu / Flea Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.87449,"lo":173.01231,"d":8504,"ds":"Pōhatu / Flea Bay is a bay located approximately 2.7 nautical miles (5.0 km; 3.1 mi) east-northeast of the entrance to Akaroa Harbour in New Zealand. It is home to the largest penguin colony on Banks Peninsula and the Pōhatu Marine Reserve. The marine reserve is habitat for many fish species including moki, butterfish, guppy, banded wrasse, blue cod, leather jackets, lobsters, pāua and rockfish.","wpu":"Pōhatu / Flea Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Banks_Peninsula_Track_0310.jpg/960px-Banks_Peninsula_Track_0310.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Akaroa Marine Reserve","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.882367,"lo":172.965131,"d":8605,"ds":"Akaroa Marine Reserve is a no-take marine protected area covering 512.15 hectares (1,265.6 acres) at the entrance to Akaroa Harbour in New Zealand. The marine reserve was approved in 2013 after a lengthy campaign, and established in 2014. The adjacent Akaroa Taiāpure occupies the rest of the harbour and was established earlier.","wpu":"Akaroa Marine Reserve","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Akaroa_Harbour_entrance%2C_Canterbury%2C_New_Zealand%2C_22nd._Nov._2010_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg/960px-Akaroa_Harbour_entrance%2C_Canterbury%2C_New_Zealand%2C_22nd._Nov._2010_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pōhatu Marine Reserve","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.878222,"lo":173.019376,"d":9125,"ds":"Pōhatu Marine Reserve is a 215 ha (530 acres) marine reserve centred on Pōhatu / Flea Bay and lies between Ounu-hau Point and Redcliffe Point on Banks Peninsula in the Canterbury region of New Zealand. It was formally notified in 1999 and is administered by the Department of Conservation (DOC).","wpu":"Pōhatu Marine Reserve","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Banks_Peninsula_Track_0310.jpg/960px-Banks_Peninsula_Track_0310.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Akaroa is a small town on Banks Peninsula in the Canterbury Region of the South Island of New Zealand, situated within a harbour of the same name. The name Akaroa is Kāi Tahu Māori for \"Long Harbour\", which would be spelled Whangaroa in standard Māori. The area was also named Port Louis-Philippe by French settlers after the reigning French king Louis Philippe I. The town is 84 kilometres (52 mi) by road from Christchurch and is the terminus of State Highway 75.","wpu":"Akaroa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/AkaroaDec032024_01.jpg/960px-AkaroaDec032024_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}